      "cite": "eq-gamma-products",
      "prompt": "<p>Verify the identities above using $\\kGamma(x+1) = x \\kGamma(x)$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "Take $x = k+p$ in $\\kGamma(x+1) = x\\kGamma(x)$: $\\kGamma(k+p+1) = (k+p)\\kGamma(k+p)$. Take $x = k-1+p$: $\\kGamma(k+p) = (k-1+p)\\kGamma(k-1+p)$, so $\\kGamma(k+p+1) = (k+p)(k-1+p)\\kGamma(k-1+p)$. Each use of the property peels off one factor and lowers the argument by one, so after $k$ uses the argument is down to $1+p$: $\\kGamma(k+p+1) = (k+p)(k-1+p)\\cdots(2+p)(1+p)\\kGamma(1+p)$. The second identity is the same computation with $-p$ in place of $p$, since the property holds wherever $\\kGamma$ is defined. Check: for $k = 3$, $\\kGamma(4+p) - (3+p)(2+p)(1+p)\\kGamma(1+p)$ simplifies to $0$ in SymPy."
      }
