      "cite": "frobenius-steps",
      "prompt": "<p>Find a particular (Frobenius-type) solution of $x^2 \\kydprime + x \\kyprime + (1+x) \\ky = 0$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "Put $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ into the equation. The part $x^2\\kydprime + x\\kyprime + \\ky$ is $\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\bigl((k+\\kr)^2 + 1\\bigr)\\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ and $x\\ky = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\ka_{k-1} x^{k+\\kr}$, so the lowest power gives the indicial equation $\\kr^2 + 1 = 0$, with the complex roots $\\kr = \\pm i$: case (vi). For $k \\ge 1$, $\\bigl((k+\\kr)^2 + 1\\bigr)\\ka_k + \\ka_{k-1} = 0$; with $\\kr = i$, $(k+i)^2 + 1 = k(k+2i)$, so $\\ka_k = -\\frac{\\ka_{k-1}}{k(k+2i)}$. With $\\ka_0 = 1$: $\\ka_1 = -\\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{2}{5}i$, $\\ka_2 = -\\frac{1}{40} - \\frac{3}{40}i$, $\\ka_3 = \\frac{3}{520} + \\frac{7}{1560}i$, and so on. As $x^{i} = e^{i\\ln x} = \\cos(\\ln x) + i\\sin(\\ln x)$, the real part of $x^{i}\\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^k$ is a real solution for $x > 0$: $\\ky = \\cos(\\ln x)\\Bigl(1 - \\frac{x}{5} - \\frac{x^2}{40} + \\cdots\\Bigr) - \\sin(\\ln x)\\Bigl(\\frac{2x}{5} - \\frac{3x^2}{40} + \\cdots\\Bigr)$ (the imaginary part is a second one). Summed to 14 terms, this real part satisfies the equation to within $10^{-15}$ at $x = 0.5$ and $x = 2$."
      }
    },
