      "cite": "frobenius-steps",
      "prompt": "<p>Find a particular (Frobenius-type) solution of $x \\kydprime - \\ky = 0$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "Put $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ in: $x\\kydprime = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty (k+\\kr)(k+\\kr-1)\\ka_k x^{k+\\kr-1}$ and $\\ky = \\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\ka_{k-1} x^{k+\\kr-1}$. The lowest power, $x^{\\kr-1}$, gives the indicial equation $\\kr(\\kr-1) = 0$, with roots $1$ and $0$ differing by an integer (case (v)); take the larger, $\\kr = 1$. For $k \\ge 1$, $(k+1)k\\,\\ka_k = \\ka_{k-1}$, so $\\ka_k = \\frac{\\ka_{k-1}}{k(k+1)}$, and with $\\ka_0 = 1$, $\\ka_k = \\frac{1}{k!\\,(k+1)!}$. A Frobenius-type solution is $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{k+1}}{k!\\,(k+1)!} = x + \\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{12} + \\frac{x^4}{144} + \\cdots$. (The root $\\kr = 0$ gives no series of this form: at $k = 1$ it would need $0 \\cdot \\ka_1 = \\ka_0$.) Substituting the first twelve terms back leaves only a term in $x^{12}$."
      }
