      "cite": "frobenius-steps",
      "prompt": "<p>Find a particular (Frobenius-type) solution of $\\kydprime +\\frac{1}{x}\\kyprime - x\\ky = 0$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "Multiply by $x^2$: $x^2\\kydprime + x\\kyprime - x^3\\ky = 0$. With $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$, $x^2\\kydprime + x\\kyprime = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty (k+\\kr)^2\\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ and $x^3\\ky = \\sum_{k=3}^\\infty \\ka_{k-3} x^{k+\\kr}$. The indicial equation is $\\kr^2 = 0$, a double root $\\kr = 0$ (case (iv)). Then $\\ka_1 = \\ka_2 = 0$ and $k^2\\ka_k = \\ka_{k-3}$ for $k \\ge 3$, so only every third coefficient survives: $\\ka_{3n} = \\frac{\\ka_{3n-3}}{9n^2}$, and with $\\ka_0 = 1$, $\\ka_{3n} = \\frac{1}{9^n (n!)^2}$. A solution is $\\ky = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{x^{3n}}{9^n (n!)^2} = 1 + \\frac{x^3}{9} + \\frac{x^6}{324} + \\cdots$; substituting the first six terms back leaves only a term in $x^{18}$."
