        "solution": "Multiply by $x$: $2x^2\\kydprime + x\\kyprime - x^3\\ky = 0$. With $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$, $2x^2\\kydprime + x\\kyprime = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty (k+\\kr)(2k+2\\kr-1)\\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ and $x^3\\ky = \\sum_{k=3}^\\infty \\ka_{k-3} x^{k+\\kr}$. The indicial equation is $\\kr(2\\kr-1) = 0$, with roots $\\krone = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\krtwo = 0$, which do not differ by an integer (case (iii)). For $k \\ge 1$, $(k+\\kr)(2k+2\\kr-1)\\ka_k = \\ka_{k-3}$ (with $\\ka_{-2} = \\ka_{-1} = 0$), so $\\ka_1 = \\ka_2 = 0$ and only every third coefficient survives. For $\\kr = 0$, $k(2k-1)\\ka_k = \\ka_{k-3}$ gives $\\ka_3 = \\frac{1}{15}$, $\\ka_6 = \\frac{1}{990}$; for $\\kr = \\frac{1}{2}$, $k(2k+1)\\kb_k = \\kb_{k-3}$ gives $\\kb_3 = \\frac{1}{21}$, $\\kb_6 = \\frac{1}{1638}$. The general solution, for $x > 0$, is $\\ky = A\\Bigl(1 + \\frac{x^3}{15} + \\frac{x^6}{990} + \\cdots\\Bigr) + B\\,x^{1/2}\\Bigl(1 + \\frac{x^3}{21} + \\frac{x^6}{1638} + \\cdots\\Bigr)$; substituting either series back leaves only powers beyond those kept."
