      "prompt": "<p>Find the general solution of $x^2 \\kydprime - x \\kyprime -\\ky = 0$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "With $\\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$ every term keeps its power: $x^2\\kydprime - x\\kyprime - \\ky = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\bigl((k+\\kr)(k+\\kr-1) - (k+\\kr) - 1\\bigr)\\ka_k x^{k+\\kr}$. The indicial equation is $\\kr^2 - 2\\kr - 1 = 0$, so $\\kr = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{2}$, two roots differing by $2\\sqrt{2}$, not an integer (case (iii)). For $k \\ge 1$ the factor $(k+\\kr)^2 - 2(k+\\kr) - 1$ is not zero (that would make $k+\\kr$ another root), so every $\\ka_k$ with $k \\ge 1$ is zero and each series is a single power. The general solution is $\\ky = A x^{1+\\sqrt{2}} + B x^{1-\\sqrt{2}}$ for $x > 0$; each power satisfies the equation exactly."
