      "cite": "regular-singular",
      "prompt": "<p>In the following equations classify the point $x=0$ as <em>ordinary</em>, <em>regular singular</em>, or <em>singular but not regular singular</em>.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>$x^2(1+x^2)\\kydprime+x\\ky=0$</li><li>$x^2\\kydprime+\\kyprime+\\ky=0$</li><li>$x\\kydprime+x^3\\kyprime+\\ky=0$</li><li>$x\\kydprime+x\\kyprime-e^x\\ky=0$</li><li>$x^2\\kydprime+x^2\\kyprime+x^2\\ky=0$</li></ol>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "In each equation $P(0) = 0$, so $x = 0$ is singular, and the two limits $\\lim_{x\\to 0} x\\frac{Q(x)}{P(x)}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0} x^2\\frac{R(x)}{P(x)}$ decide. a) Regular singular: $x\\frac{Q}{P} = 0$ and $x^2\\frac{R}{P} = \\frac{x}{1+x^2} \\to 0$. b) Singular but not regular singular: $x\\frac{Q}{P} = \\frac{1}{x}$ has no limit. c) Regular singular: $x\\frac{Q}{P} = x^3 \\to 0$ and $x^2\\frac{R}{P} = x \\to 0$. d) Regular singular: $x\\frac{Q}{P} = x \\to 0$ and $x^2\\frac{R}{P} = -xe^x \\to 0$. e) Regular singular by the definition as stated: $P(0) = 0$, and $x\\frac{Q}{P} = x$ and $x^2\\frac{R}{P} = x^2$ both go to $0$. Dividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives $\\kydprime + \\kyprime + \\ky = 0$, for which $0$ is an ordinary point: the singularity comes only from a factor common to all three coefficients."
